},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1957_AMC_12_Q34","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"substitute-line-equation","label":"Substitute the line equation and solve a quadratic inequality","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-line-equation":["cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_202","cnm_146","cnm_150"],"id":"substitute-and-factor","label":"Substitute and factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-and-factor":["cnm_094","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_094","cnm_h024","cnm_202","cnm_146","cnm_150"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. Infinitely many","reason":"The line's distance from the origin is 15/√34<4, so it crosses the disk in a nondegenerate segment. That segment contains infinitely many real ordered pairs.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["Interprets number-pairs as real ordered pairs; no integer restriction is stated.","Number-pairs are interpreted as real ordered pairs; no integer restriction is stated."],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"substitute-and-solve-inequality","label":"Reduce to a quadratic interval","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-and-solve-inequality":["cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 3 + 2√2","reason":"The circle has x ≥ 3 - √6 > 0. Substituting y = mx gives a quadratic with discriminant -12(m² - 6m + 1), so feasible slopes satisfy 3 - 2√2 ≤ m ≤ 3 + 2√2. The upper endpoint is attained at tangency.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1984_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"slope-substitution","label":"Substitute a line and bound its slope","nodeIds":["cnm_223","cnm_h024"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h176","cnm_h167"],"id":"solution:slope-substitution:79930d1a1d62","label":"Test real intersections using a discriminant","methodDirectNodeIds":{"slope-substitution":["cnm_223","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_223","cnm_h024","cnm_h176","cnm_h167"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h176","cnm_h167","cnm_h171","cnm_065"],"id":"solution:tangent-distance:79930d1a1d62","label":"Compare the center-to-line distance with the radius","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-distance":["cnm_h174","cnm_h179","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h174","cnm_h179","cnm_h024","cnm_h176","cnm_h167","cnm_h171","cnm_065"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (12 integers)","reason":"The expression factors as (x²-1)(x²-50), which is negative exactly when 1<x²<50. Since 49<50<64, the integer solutions are ±2, ±3, ±4, ±5, ±6, ±7, giving 12.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_10B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"quadratic-substitution","label":"Solve the quadratic in x²","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quadratic-substitution":["cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_014","cnm_020"],"id":"factor-and-count","label":"Treat the expression as a quadratic in x squared","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-count":["cnm_h020","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h020","cnm_h024","cnm_014","cnm_020"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (4 ordered pairs)","reason":"Rewrite the equation as x^2020+(y−1)²=1. If x=0, then y=0 or 2. If x=±1, then y=1. Any |x|≥2 is impossible, giving exactly four pairs.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12B_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bound-integer-y","label":"Bound y by a quadratic inequality","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-integer-y":["cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"restrict-y-by-signs","label":"Restrict y first","methodDirectNodeIds":{"restrict-y-by-signs":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_h024","cnm_h020","cnm_020"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_h024","cnm_h020","cnm_020"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 4","reason":"Writing k = floor(x) gives x = (k² + 2)/3. The conditions k ≤ (k² + 2)/3 < k + 1 allow exactly k = 0, 1, 2, 3, yielding x = 2/3, 1, 2, 11/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_10B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bound-and-test-floor-values","label":"Restrict the integer part","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-and-test-floor-values":["math:floor-ceiling","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["math:floor-ceiling","cnm_h024"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 15","reason":"The initial sum is 2500. The largest k terms sum to k(100-k). For k=14 this is 1204, leaving at least 2500-2·1204=92. For k=15 it is 1275, producing 2500-2·1275=-50. Thus the minimum is 15.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10B_Q05","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"solve-sign-change-threshold","label":"Solve a quadratic inequality","methodDirectNodeIds":{"solve-sign-change-threshold":["cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (15)","reason":"The original sum is 50² = 2500, so the changed terms must sum to more than 1250. The largest possible sum of k changed terms is k(100-k). For k = 14 this is 1204, which is insufficient; for k = 15 it is 1275, giving a new total of -50. Therefore the minimum is 15.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_12B_Q05","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"solve-sign-change-threshold","label":"Solve a quadratic inequality","methodDirectNodeIds":{"solve-sign-change-threshold":["cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: necessarily x<=0 or x>=4; the exact attainable set is x<0 or x>=4","reason":"Rearrangement gives y^2-xy+x=0. Its discriminant requires x^2-4x>=0, proving A. At x=0 the only candidate is y=0, which the original equation excludes.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["Choice A states a valid necessary bound but includes the unattainable boundary value x=0."],"questionId":"1957_AMC_12_Q45","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"discriminant-in-y","label":"Use a quadratic in y","methodDirectNodeIds":{"discriminant-in-y":["cnm_223","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_223","cnm_h024"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_219"],"id":"real-roots-in-y","label":"Determine when an allowed real y exists","methodDirectNodeIds":{"real-roots-in-y":["cnm_165","cnm_223","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_165","cnm_223","cnm_h024","cnm_156","cnm_219"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, s must be greater than zero","reason":"If the roots are u,v>1, then b=-(u+v) and c=uv. Hence s=1-u-v+uv=(u-1)(v-1)>0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1959_AMC_12_Q44","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"quadratic-sign-at-one","label":"Evaluate left of both roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quadratic-sign-at-one":["cnm_h024","cnm_029"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024","cnm_029"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 1.","reason":"S=N(N+1). At N=999 it equals 999000, while at N=1000 it equals 1001000. Therefore the least N is 1000, whose digit sum is 1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1968_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"solve-quadratic-threshold","label":"Solve the quadratic threshold","methodDirectNodeIds":{"solve-quadratic-threshold":["cnm_h024","cnm_222"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024","cnm_222"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, x<-2 or -1<x<1 or x>2.","reason":"The quotient is positive when numerator and denominator share a sign: either x^2>4 or x^2<1. Strict positivity excludes numerator zeros, and the original denominator excludes x=±1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1969_AMC_12_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"split-numerator-denominator-signs","label":"Split by common signs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"split-numerator-denominator-signs":["cnm_h024","cnm_099"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024","cnm_099"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"substitute-square-variable","label":"Use t=x squared","methodDirectNodeIds":{"substitute-square-variable":["cnm_h024","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024","cnm_220"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_094","cnm_017"],"id":"same-sign-cases","label":"Match numerator and denominator signs","methodDirectNodeIds":{"same-sign-cases":["math:rational-inequalities","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["math:rational-inequalities","cnm_h024","cnm_156","cnm_094","cnm_017"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. x<-1 or -1<x<1","reason":"For x<0, the expression is (1+x)², positive except at x=-1. For x≥0, it is 1-x², positive exactly for 0≤x<1. Combining the cases gives (-∞,-1)∪(-1,1).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1976_AMC_12_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"piecewise-quadratic-analysis","label":"Remove the absolute value by cases","methodDirectNodeIds":{"piecewise-quadratic-analysis":["cnm_h024","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: x>1/4","reason":"Because x>0, dividing √x<2x by √x gives 1<2√x. This is equivalent to √x>1/2 and therefore x>1/4; equality at 1/4 is excluded.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1980_AMC_12_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"square-positive-sides","label":"Square the positive sides","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-positive-sides":["cnm_168","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_168","cnm_h024"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 2","reason":"The condition (a-2)(b-2)=ab/2 rearranges to (a-4)(b-4)=8. Valid dimensions require a>4 and b>a. The positive factor pairs with first entry smaller are (1,8) and (2,4), giving (a,b)=(5,12) and (6,8).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bound-shorter-dimension","label":"Bound and enumerate the shorter dimension","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-shorter-dimension":["cnm_h024","cnm_h020"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024","cnm_h020"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, (3+√5)/2","reason":"The conditions require b/(b-1)≤a≤b-1, so b²-3b+1≥0. Since b>2, this gives b≥(3+√5)/2. Equality is attained with a=(1+√5)/2, when both proposed triangles are degenerate.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_12B_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"quadratic-feasibility-bound","label":"Eliminate a using inequalities","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quadratic-feasibility-bound":["cnm_h024","cnm_222"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024","cnm_222"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_096","cnm_148"],"id":"triangle-feasibility","label":"Combine the two triangle restrictions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"triangle-feasibility":["cnm_111","cnm_h024","cnm_222"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_111","cnm_h024","cnm_222","cnm_096","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 18","reason":"Put x = AC. The angle bisector theorem gives BD = 30/x, so the side lengths are 10, x, and 3 + 30/x. Their triangle inequalities reduce to x > 3, x < 15, and x² − 7x + 30 > 0. The last always holds because its discriminant is −71. Thus (m,n) = (3,15), giving m + n = 18.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"side-length-inequalities","label":"Solve the triangle inequalities","methodDirectNodeIds":{"side-length-inequalities":["cnm_h024","cnm_223"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024","cnm_223"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"angle-bisector-inequalities","label":"Use the bisector and triangle inequalities","methodDirectNodeIds":{"angle-bisector-inequalities":["math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_111","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["math:angle-bisector-side-ratio","cnm_111","cnm_h024"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. 4","reason":"The equation becomes x^2020 + (y - 1)² = 1. The two nonnegative integer terms must be 0 and 1 in some order, yielding (0,0), (0,2), (1,1), and (-1,1).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_10B_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bound-and-enumerate-y","label":"Bound and enumerate y","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-and-enumerate-y":["cnm_h024","cnm_h020"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024","cnm_h020"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"restrict-y-by-signs","label":"Restrict y first","methodDirectNodeIds":{"restrict-y-by-signs":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_h024","cnm_h020","cnm_020"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_h024","cnm_h020","cnm_020"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 6","reason":"The lower bound gives (k-20)(k-50)≤0, hence 20≤k≤50. The strict upper bound gives (k-34)^2>155, so integer k must satisfy k≤21 or k≥47. Thus k is 20, 21, 47, 48, 49, or 50, yielding six distinct positive n.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_10B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"quadratic-sign-intervals","label":"Quadratic inequality intervals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quadratic-sign-intervals":["cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h024"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 4 real solutions","reason":"Let m = floor(x). The equation gives x² = 10m - 51/4, forcing m ≥ 2 and x > 0. Requiring m ≤ x < m + 1 gives m² ≤ 10m - 51/4 < (m + 1)². The allowable integers are m = 2, 6, 7, 8, each yielding exactly one solution.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1985_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h020","cnm_074"],"id":"integer-part-interval-consistency","label":"Require the root to have the chosen integer part","methodDirectNodeIds":{"integer-part-interval-consistency":["math:floor-ceiling","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["math:floor-ceiling","cnm_h024","cnm_h020","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 7/2","reason":"Substitute y=tx to obtain (2+t+3t²)x²−(11+20t)x+40=0. Its discriminant is −80t²+280t−199. The extreme slopes are the two zeros of this expression, whose sum is 280/80=7/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_12B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"complete-square-for-feasibility","label":"Complete the square and find the slope interval endpoints","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complete-square-for-feasibility":["math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h024","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h024","cnm_220"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h167","cnm_146"],"id":"slope-intersection-discriminant","label":"Find which slopes meet the ellipse","methodDirectNodeIds":{"slope-intersection-discriminant":["cnm_h196","cnm_223","cnm_h024","cnm_226"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h196","cnm_223","cnm_h024","cnm_226","cnm_h167","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (2 ordered pairs)","reason":"The equation (a-2)(b-2)=ab/2 rearranges to (a-4)(b-4)=8. With a,b>2 and b>a, the admissible factor pairs are (1,8) and (2,4), yielding (a,b)=(5,12) and (6,8).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_10B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"bound-and-check-smaller-side","label":"Bound the smaller side and check","nodeIds":["cnm_h024","cnm_h020","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 2","reason":"At an intersection, (x+y)^2=k^2+2k and (x-y)^2=k^2-2k. Conversely, nonnegative values on both right sides construct real x and y. Thus intersection occurs exactly for k<=-2, k=0, or k>=2. Only k=-1 and k=1 fail, giving two integer values.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_12A_Q13","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"id":"sum-and-difference-squares","label":"Squares of the sum and difference","nodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_074","cnm_h024"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_020","cnm_h017"],"id":"solution:sum-and-difference-squares:54c479547abe","label":"Use squares of the sum and difference","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sum-and-difference-squares":["cnm_148","cnm_074","cnm_h024","cnm_h020"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_074","cnm_h024","cnm_h020","cnm_020","cnm_h017"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 12","reason":"The integer values of x-2 are -4, -3, -2, 2, 3, 4. Thus x is -2, -1, 0, 4, 5, 6, whose sum is 12.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_10B_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"intersect-quadratic-sign-intervals","label":"Intersect quadratic sign intervals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"intersect-quadratic-sign-intervals":["cnm_152","cnm_h024","cnm_h008"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_152","cnm_h024","cnm_h008"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 11","reason":"The condition is (x - 5)^2 + (-x - 5)^2 <= 100, which simplifies to 2x^2 + 50 <= 100. Thus -5 <= x <= 5, giving 11 integers including both endpoints.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_10B_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"circle-inequality","label":"Coordinate inequality","methodDirectNodeIds":{"circle-inequality":["cnm_h176","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h176","cnm_h024"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 199","reason":"The equation implies x²=10000(x−⌊x⌋), hence −100<x<100. Let g(x)=x−x²/10000. It is strictly increasing on this interval and has image (−101,99). Each integer k from −100 through 98 gives exactly one x with g(x)=k; since 0≤x−k=x²/10000<1, its floor is indeed k. There are 199 such integers.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"solve-on-floor-intervals","label":"Quadratic roots on floor intervals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"solve-on-floor-intervals":["cnm_222","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_222","cnm_h024"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: -3<x<0 or x>2","reason":"The difference is x+1-6/x=((x+3)(x-2))/x. It is positive exactly on (-3,0) and (2,∞), excluding the zeros and x=0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_241_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sign-split","label":"Split according to the sign of x","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sign-split":["cnm_161","cnm_096","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_161","cnm_096","cnm_h024"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 2+√2","reason":"Take A=(0,0), B=(1,0), C=(1,1), and D=(0,1). The condition becomes x²+(y+1)²=2. This circle has center (0,−1), radius √2, and center-to-D distance 2, so its greatest distance from D is 2+√2, attained at (0,−1−√2).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1983_AMC_12_Q29","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bound-the-distance-algebraically","label":"Coordinate inequality","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-the-distance-algebraically":["cnm_h177","cnm_h024","cnm_096"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h177","cnm_h024","cnm_096"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 80","reason":"The line is y=m(x-20)+14, so intersections satisfy x^2-mx+20m-14=0. There are no real intersections exactly when m^2-80m+56<0. The endpoints r and s are the two roots of this quadratic in m, giving r+s=80.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2014_AMC_12B_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h169"],"id":"solution:intersection-discriminant:d7cccd13e3bf","label":"Test the intersection equation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"intersection-discriminant":["cnm_h025","cnm_223","cnm_226","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h025","cnm_223","cnm_226","cnm_h024","cnm_h169"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h169","cnm_148","cnm_074"],"id":"minimum-gap","label":"Find the minimum vertical difference","methodDirectNodeIds":{"minimum-gap":["cnm_h025","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_235","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h025","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_235","cnm_h024","cnm_h169","cnm_148","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 199","reason":"The equation gives x²=10000(x−floor(x)), so |x|<100 and n=floor(x) lies from −100 to 99. On each interval [n,n+1], f_n(t)=t²−10000t+10000n strictly decreases. Its endpoint values are n² and (n+1)²−10000. Exactly one admissible root occurs for each n=−100,...,98, including x=0 when n=0. For n=99 the only endpoint zero is the excluded upper endpoint 100. Thus there are 199 roots.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h024"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_10B_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"quadratic-formula-filter","label":"Quadratic formula with floor-interval filtering","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quadratic-formula-filter":["cnm_222","cnm_h024"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_222","cnm_h024"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:b9bd944febf30d8590fe47b8"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 2 values: a=0 and a=-4","reason":"For a>0 the domain includes negative numbers, unlike the range. For 